# Keep the working tree LF, on every machine.
#
# core.autocrlf is `true` on this machine, which rewrites text files to CRLF on
# checkout. That is exactly the thing that broke the deadline parser: several
# parsers here are hand-written and split on "\n", so a CRLF file reads as lines
# with a trailing \r and every regex quietly matches nothing.
#
# It also matters for hosting: the daily job will run on a Linux GitHub Actions
# runner, and a repo whose contents change shape depending on which OS checked it
# out is a bug waiting for a different machine to find.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides autocrlf for the working tree, so a fresh clone here and a
# checkout on the runner produce byte-identical files — and test_line_endings.py
# stays meaningful rather than failing on everyone else's clone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell scripts are Windows-only and nothing in this repo parses them.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never normalise these.
*.db binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
